titre$Sarcoscypha-Baral
com$Adapt GF mars 2015
rem$LANGUE$Anglais
1,0$ source: /Baral/CA%20Homepage/Sarcoscypha_keys.htm
1,0$. Table of diagnostic characters (* = living state) $$f"sarcoscypha_Baral_table.pdf"
1,0$

1,0 $ Ascospores *(17-)19-21 µm long, containing several partly rather large LBs 1-4 µm diam., poles slightly truncate, nuclear region constantly lateral, without emvelope, conidia of anamorph produced on conidiophores emerging from ascospores, *5.5-6.5 x 3 µm, bi- or multiguttulate, apothecia 2.5-5 mm diam., continental and atlantic N-America, e.g. on Acer ... $ S. occidentalis
1,2 $ Ascospores min. 21-30 µm long, nuclear region more central, apothecia larger, temperate Europe and N-America ..
1,3 $ Ascospores in front view truncated or distinctly indented (notched) at the ends, in older apothecia with or without conidia produced on conidiophores emerging from the ascospores, hairs often wavy (sinuous) to coiled or curled ..
2,4 $ Ascospores in front view at best in dead material in some spores very slightly truncated at the ends, never indented, in older apothecia ascospores never forming conidia, hairs more or less straight ..
3,0 $ Living mature ascospores containing one 5-6.5(-7.5) µm large LB near each pole (somtimes also two to four (2-)3-5(-6) µm large LBs), usually with an evanescent mucilaginous envelope surrounding the entire spore (living material!), envelope within living asci compressed by forming caps at the spore ends and partly also on the sides, conidia only formed on mycelium, never in hymenium, *7-15 x 2.5-3.5 µm, containing a few small LBs near each end (low lipid content), excipular hairs straight or sinuous to coiled, montaneous Europe, highly basic soil, restricted to Tilia ... $ S. jurana
3,0 $ Living mature ascospores with c. 10-20 medium-sized LBs 1.5-3.5(-4.5) µm diam. grouped near each pole, usually with a small mucilaginous cap on each pole (living material!), no envelope on the sides, in older apothecia germinating by (1-)2(-4) conidiophores that produce conidia *((?8.5-))(11-)12-16(-19)((-22)) x (4-)4.5-5.5(-6.5) µm, multiguttulate (high lipid content), excipular hairs strikingly irregularly sinuous to curled, colline to subalpine-boreal Europe & central to eastern N-America, slightly basic soil, mainly on Alnus, Salix, Acer, Robinia ... $ S. austriaca
4,0 $ Ascospores predominantly *22-33 x 9-11.5 µm, larger LBs 0.5-1.5(-2) µm diam., ascocarps 6-20 mm diam., Macaronesia, mainly on Lauraceae ... $ S. macaronesica
4,5 $ Ascospores predominantly *25-40 x 11-12-14 µm, ascocarps 10-90 mm diam. ..
5,0 $ Ascospores mature always without mucilaginous envelope, larger LBs 1-3 µm diam., apothecial margin often finely hairy or crenulate, atlantic Europe, mainly on Rosaceae, Fagus, Corylus, Ulmus (in mediterranean on Quercus with slightly smaller spores) ... $ S. coccinea
5,6 $ Ascospores with an entire envelope forming rather large polar mucilaginous caps, N-America ..
6,0 $ with several small LBs (1.5-3.5 µm), ozeanic N-America, mainly on Quercus … $ S. aff. coccinea
6,0 $ with one or several medium large LBs c. (2-)4-5(-6) µm diam., north-atlantic N-America, mainly on Tilia ... $ S. dudleyi
